#{[#edb/ref 17592186048777 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065017} :activity/id #uuid "313df706-051c-53f8-a9d2-078969d05bee" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065017 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065409 :tutorial/content "We know that the derivative of sine is cosine:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}(\\sin{x}) = \\cos{x}\n$$\n\nSince integration is the reverse of differentiation, we see that the integral of cosine is sine:\n\n$$\n\\int \\cos{x}\\,\\textrm d x = \\sin{x} + C\n$$\n\nWe can discover two other integrals by considering the derivatives of cosine and tangent:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sin}x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{cos}x+C \\\\ \\int {sec}^{2}x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{tan}x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, by considering the derivatives of secant, cosecant, and cotangent, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{sec}x+C \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{csc}x\\operatorname{cot}x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{csc}x+C \\\\ \\int {csc}^{2}x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{cot}x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith these six formulas, we can integrate many different trigonometric functions." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065018}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065018 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064178 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064052 :question/id #uuid "a9671d61-6b85-5d84-b49c-a6443569f6c0" :question/math-academy-id "e-382" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int 3\\sin{x}\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int \\sin x \\, \\textrm dx = -\\cos x + C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3sin⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\int sin⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3(-cos⁡x)+C \\\\  & =-3cos⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Sine and Cosine"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065019}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065019 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064179 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064053 :question/id #uuid "4c3342aa-dd1c-566b-9229-164e594c554c" :question/math-academy-id "e-383" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int (-\\csc x\\cot x)\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int \\csc x \\cot x \\, \\textrm dx = - \\csc x + C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (-csc⁡xcot⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int (csc⁡xcot⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-(-csc⁡x)+C \\\\  & =csc⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Secant-Tangent and Cosecant-Cotangent"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065020}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065020 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064180 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064054 :question/id #uuid "3d2bde25-f8dc-5e62-9999-6593424f0ae2" :question/math-academy-id "e-384" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\displaystyle{\\int} (2\\sec^2 x + \\sec x \\tan x )\\, \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\int \\sec^2 x \\, \\textrm dx = \\tan x + C\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\int \\sec x \\tan x \\, \\textrm dx = \\sec x + C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\int (2\\sec^2 x + \\sec x \\tan x )\\, \\textrm{d}x& = 2 \\int \\sec^2 x \\, \\textrm{d}x+ \\int \\sec x \\tan x \\, \\textrm{d}x \\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&=2\\tan x + \\sec x + C.\n\n}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Secant Squared"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065021}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065021 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064181 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064055 :question/id #uuid "c2c57145-3988-5ce8-b13f-114bba6964f7" :question/math-academy-id "e-385" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\displaystyle{\\int} \\csc{x}(\\csc{x}+3\\cot{x} )\\, \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\int \\csc^2 x \\, \\textrm dx = -\\cot x + C\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\int \\csc x \\cot x \\, \\textrm dx = -\\csc x + C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int csc⁡x(csc⁡x+3cot⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {csc}^{2}⁡x+3csc⁡xcot⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {csc}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+3\\int csc⁡xcot⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-cot⁡x-3csc⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Cosecant Squared"}}] :activity/title "Integrating Trigonometric Functions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064392}] [#edb/ref 17592186048569 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088817} :activity/id #uuid "115bfa9d-34ae-51ca-aa04-b0f23cf266a2" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088817 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088859 :tutorial/content "Recall that a first-order linear ODE can always be expressed in the standard form\n\n$$\ny'(x)+P(x)y(x) =Q(x).\n$$\n\nFirst-order linear ODEs are special because we can always find the general solution using an **integrating factor.** \n\nAn integrating factor is a function $I(x)$ that allows us to rewrite the left-hand side as an exact derivative after multiplying the equation by $I(x).$ The resulting equation can then be solved by integrating both sides.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the following first-order linear ODE.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + \\frac{2}{x}\\, y = 3\n$$\n\nAs it stands, this differential equation cannot be integrated directly. However, if we multiply both sides of the equation by $I(x) = x^2,$ we get\n\n$$\nx^2\\cdot \\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + x^2\\cdot \\frac{2}{x}\\, y = x^2\\cdot 3\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as follows:\n\n$$\nx^2 \\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + 2 x y = 3x^2 \\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nNow, here's the neat part. Provided our integrating factor is correct, we can *always* write the left-hand side of the equation as the exact derivative\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}\\left(y\\cdot I(x)\\right)\n$$\n\nwhich in our case is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}\\left(y \\cdot x^2\\right).\n$$\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}( y \\cdot x^2) = 3x^2.\n$$\n\nWe can now find the general solution by integrating both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot {x}^{2}) & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot {x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y\\cdot {x}^{2} & ={x}^{3}+C \\\\ y=x+\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can check that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}( y \\cdot x^2) = 3x^2\n$$\n\nis indeed equivalent to the original equation $(\\ast)$ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot {x}^{2}) & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)\\cdot {x}^{2}+y\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\cdot 2x & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy & =3{x}^{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy finding an appropriate integrating factor, we can solve any first-order linear ODE! So the question is, how do we find an integrating factor? Let's discuss this next." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088818}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088818 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088860 :tutorial/content "Given a first-order linear ODE, we can find an integrating factor and use it to solve the equation using the following procedure.\n\n- **Step 1**: Write the equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\n- **Step 2**: Compute an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition\n\n$$\nI(x) = e^{\\int P(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x} \\,.\n$$\n\n- **Step 3**: Multiply both sides of the equation found in step 1 by $I(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nI(x)\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + I(x)P(x)y = I(x)Q(x)\n$$\n\n- **Step 4**: Rewrite the left side as \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\Bigl(y I(x)\\Bigr)\n$$\n\nIf steps 1 through 3 are done correctly, then we can always do this, and we can check that it's correct by differentiating. Our new equation is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\Bigl(y I(x)\\Bigr) = I(x)Q(x)\n$$\n\n- **Step 5**: Integrate both sides of the equation with respect to $x$ and solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yI(x))\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int I(x)Q(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ yI(x) & =\\int I(x)Q(x)\\,\\text{d}x+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{I(x)}(\\int I(x)Q(x)\\,\\text{d}x+C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nYou might be wondering where the formula for $I(x)$ comes from; we'll discuss this at the end of the lesson. First, let's apply this method to a concrete example in the next slide." :tutorial/title "The Integrating Factor Method"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088819}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088819 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087877 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087452 :question/id #uuid "1e3c0253-226d-596c-a831-7c68ec76b21c" :question/math-academy-id "e-21741" :question/problem "Consider the following first-order linear ODE for the function $y = y(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + 2y = 2e^{-2x}\n$$\n\nWrite the equation in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(y I(x) \\right) = I(x)Q(x),$ where $I(x)$ is an integrating factor." :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x) = 2$ and $Q(x) = 2e^{-2x}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 2\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x) = e^{2x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y) & ={e}^{2x}\\cdot 2{e}^{-2x} \\\\ {e}^{2x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{e}^{2x}\\cdot y & ={e}^{2x}\\cdot 2{e}^{-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(y I(x)\\right){:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{2x}) & ={e}^{2x}\\cdot 2{e}^{-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Transforming First-Order Linear ODEs Using Integrating Factors"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088820}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088820 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087878 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087453 :question/id #uuid "89446fd7-75c2-5da5-bd97-469100f3ca7b" :question/math-academy-id "e-21742" :question/problem "Find the general solution of the following differential equation using the method of integrating factors.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac {3} {x} y = \\dfrac{\\cos x}{x^2}, \\qquad x \\gt 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x) = \\dfrac{3}{x}$ and $Q(x) = \\dfrac{\\cos x}{x^2}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (3/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})} \\\\  & ={x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x) = x^3$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{x}y) & ={x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3{x}^{2}\\cdot y & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(y\\cdot I(x)\\right)$ and carry out the integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{3}) & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{3})\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{3} & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this integral, we integrate by parts. \n\n$$\n\\int uv'\\,\\textrm d x = uv - \\int u' v\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nLet $u = x, u' = 1, v' = \\cos x,$ and $v = \\sin x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y{x}^{3} & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{3} & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{3} & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{C}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using Integrating Factors"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088821}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088821 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087879 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087454 :question/id #uuid "09d09b0b-2132-59b2-9eb9-ae0748f09252" :question/math-academy-id "e-21743" :question/problem "Solve the following initial value problem using the method of integrating factors.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + 3x^2y = 9x^2, \\qquad y(0) = 1\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith $P(x) = 3x^2$ and $Q(x) = 9x^2.$\n\nWe compute an integrating factor using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation in standard form by $I(x) = e^{x^3}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3{x}^{2}y) & ={e}^{{x}^{3}}\\cdot 9{x}^{2} \\\\ {e}^{{x}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\cdot y & =9{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{{x}^{3}}) & =9{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x,$ making use of the substitution $u = x^3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{{x}^{3}})\\text{d}x & =\\int 9{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{e}^{{x}^{3}} & =\\int 3{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ y{e}^{{x}^{3}} & =3{e}^{u}+C \\\\ y{e}^{{x}^{3}} & =3{e}^{{x}^{3}}+C \\\\ y & =3+C{e}^{-{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the general solution. To find the value of $C$ corresponding to the specific solution, we apply the initial condition $y(0) = 1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =3+C{e}^{-{0}^{3}} \\\\ 1 & =3+C \\\\ C & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = 3 - 2e^{-x^3}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Initial Value Problems Using Integrating Factors"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088822}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088822 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088861 :tutorial/content "We will now derive the formula for the integrating factor. \n\nConsider the first-order linear equation in standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x).\n$$\n\nOur goal is to rewrite the left-hand side as the derivative of a product.\n\nTo do that, we multiply both sides of the equation by some function $I(x)$ that we wish to determine.\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}I(x)\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + {\\color{purple}I(x)P(x)}y} = I(x)Q(x)\n$$\n\nWe want the left-hand side to match the derivative of $y\\cdot I(x)$. But by the product rule,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\Bigl(y\\cdot I(x)\\Bigr)\n=\n{\\color{blue}I(x)\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + {\\color{purple}I'(x)}y}.\n$$\n\nSo, if we choose $I(x)$ so that\n\n$$\nI'(x) = I(x)P(x),\n$$\n\nthen the left-hand side becomes $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\Bigl(y\\cdot I(x)\\Bigr).$\n\nDividing both sides of $I'(x) = I(x)P(x)$ by $I(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{I'(x)}{I(x)} = P(x).\n$$\n\nNext, integrating both sides with respect to $x$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{I}^{′}(x)}{I(x)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(I(x)))\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(I(x)) & =\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x+C \\\\ I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x+C} \\\\  & ={e}^{C}\\cdot {e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, because multiplying $I(x)$ by a nonzero constant does not change the method, we can choose $e^C = 1$ and define an integrating factor as\n\n$$\nI(x) = e^{\\int P(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$" :tutorial/title "Deriving the Integrating Factor"}}] :activity/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using Integrating Factors" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087942}] [#edb/ref 17592186049380 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097555} :activity/id #uuid "769e6c1f-6b03-536e-94bf-1afd7f78a164" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097555 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097933 :tutorial/content "Often, differential equations aren't easy to solve. Some are so difficult that they can only be solved using entire rooms full of very powerful computers! \n\nHowever, there is one type that's often quite easy. These are differential equations that can be written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = g(x).\n$$\n\nAs an example, suppose that we want to find the solution to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{1}{1+2x}.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this differential equation by integrating both sides:\n\n$$\n\\int\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\,\\textrm d x = \\int\\dfrac{1}{1+2x}\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nFor the left-hand side, calculating the indefinite integral of $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$ gives $y+C_1$ because differentiation is reversed by integration. For the right-hand side, this is a reasonably standard integral that we know how to compute. So, we get\n\n$$\ny + C_1 = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln|1+2x| + C_2.\n$$\n\nWe now tidy up the result, solving for $y$ and combining the two constants $C_1$ and $C_2$ into a third constant $C=C_2-C_1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+{C}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}|1+2x|+{C}_{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}|1+2x|+({C}_{2}-{C}_{1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}|1+2x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution we obtained is called a **general solution** because it involves the arbitrary constant $C.$ This is in contrast to \"particular\" solutions, in which the constant is set to a fixed value. We will learn more about particular solutions later, but for now we will focus on general solutions.\n\n**Note:** Remember that we combined the constants $C_1$ and $C_2$ into a single constant $C.$ For these types of differential equations, we can usually do this, and for that reason, we often go straight to adding a constant $C$ to one side of the equation." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097556}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097556 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096696 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096496 :question/id #uuid "e8b9014e-32cf-5f04-856c-7fd663bea50f" :question/math-academy-id "e-528" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation $f'(x) - 3\\sin(2x) = 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\nf'(x) = 3\\sin{2x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(x) & =3\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution of a Differential Equation Using Integration"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097557}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097557 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096697 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096497 :question/id #uuid "a8e6c58c-da62-5dab-8325-a53c14d9b8ba" :question/math-academy-id "e-529" :question/problem "Solve the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d^2 y}{\\textrm d x^2} = x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+C)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{3}+Cx+D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$ We cannot combine them, because $C$ is being multiplied by $x$ while $D$ is not."} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution of a Second Order Differential Equation by Integrating Twice"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097558}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097558 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096698 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096498 :question/id #uuid "ab40a4d3-c418-5b64-a04c-bb017155a4d2" :question/math-academy-id "e-530" :question/problem "Solve the differential equation $y'  = 2x,$ and sketch the solution curves of this equation." :question/worked-solution "Integrating the equation, we get\n\n$$\ny = x^2+C.\n$$\n\nWe can now plot the solution curves by selecting different values for $C.$ The sketch below shows the solution curves for $C=-2,0,1.$\n\n![](images/e3/e37771799ef5fadc259e6ea0073042a83e0974b5a2619d1af3e87a7467e6d780.png)"} :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Solution Curves of a Differential Equation"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097559}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097559 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097934 :tutorial/content "Often, instead of the general solution that involves an arbitrary constant, we want a **particular solution**. A particular solution is a solution that satisfies the differential equation *and* some other condition on the function.  \n\nAs a simple example, consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx} = 2x.\n$$\n\nWe can find the general solution by integrating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & ={x}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn its current form, the function $y$ represents infinitely many solutions, each with a different value of the arbitrary constant $C.$ \n\nHowever, suppose we want to find a **particular solution** that satisfies the condition $y(3)=10.$ To do this, we can solve for the appropriate value of the arbitrary constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={x}^{2}+C \\\\ 10 & ={3}^{2}+C \\\\ 10 & =9+C \\\\ 1 & =C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the desired particular solution is\n\n$$\ny = x^2 + 1.\n$$" :tutorial/title "Particular Solutions of a Differential Equation"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097560}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097560 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096699 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096499 :question/id #uuid "29a0265e-ad88-5ac9-88fe-878aa4f0d0bf" :question/math-academy-id "e-531" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation below that satisfies the condition $f(0)=2.$\n\n$$\nf'(x) - \\dfrac{3}{1+x^2} = 0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\dfrac{3}{1+x^2}.\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{3}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(x) & =3\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our general solution is $f(x) = 3\\arctan{x} + C.$ To find a particular solution, we substitute the condition $f(0) = 2$ into our solution and obtain a value for $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+C \\\\ 2 & =3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)+C \\\\ 2 & =0+C \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is \n\n$$\nf(x)=3\\arctan x + 2.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Solution of a Differential Equation With a Given Condition"}}] :activity/title "Solving First-Order ODEs Using Direct Integration" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096863}] [#edb/ref 17592186049486 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101706} :activity/id #uuid "55a62405-baa9-5145-b156-afa7cad6d735" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101706 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186102031 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 2y.\n$$\n\nHow can we verify whether a given function is a **solution** of this equation? For example, is $y=e^{2x}$ a solution?\n\nTo check whether a given function $y$ satisfies a differential equation, we calculate the derivative and plug the result into the equation.\n\nFor $y=e^{2x},$ the derivative is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 2e^{2x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe now substitute the above and our expression for $y,$ into the original equation and see if we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2y \\\\ {{2{e}^{2x}}_{⏟}}_{{y}^{'}} & =2\\cdot {{{e}^{2x}}_{⏟}}_{y} \\\\ 2{e}^{2x} & =2{e}^{2x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe get a true statement! Therefore, $y=e^{2x}$ is a solution to the original equation.\n\n**Note:** There are often infinitely many solutions to a given differential equation! We will learn how to solve differential equations like the one shown above in future lessons." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101707}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101707 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100888 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100725 :question/id #uuid "e092e191-84ac-5d9f-b0db-94d628a6154e" :question/math-academy-id "e-525" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\ny' = -\\dfrac{y}{x}, \\qquad x \\neq 0\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f'(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$\n\n- Substituting $y = f(x)$ and $y' = f'(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(\\frac{1}{x})}^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ and $y' = -\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Solution to a First-Order Differential Equation"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101812}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101795 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100951 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100795 :question/id #uuid "f5ffa549-e77e-5db4-9fde-5b0134957539" :question/math-academy-id "e-8732" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{3y}{x}, \\qquad y(-1) = -2.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y = Ax^3$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y = Ax^3$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(-1) = -2.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y({\\color{red}{-1}}) = {\\color{blue}{-2}}$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{x}^{3} \\\\ -2 & =A(-1)^{3} \\\\ -2 & =A(-1) \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = 2x^3 .\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Initial Value Problems"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101812 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100959 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100801 :question/id #uuid "1228900d-87d4-5ff0-8e01-ff9417e82220" :question/math-academy-id "e-9978" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = y.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y = e^x + 1$\n\n- $y = -e^x$\n\n- $y = 2e^x$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y = e^x + 1,$ we get $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = e^x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ {e}^{x} & \\overset{?}{=}{e}^{x}+1 \\\\ 0 & \\ne 1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *false*. Therefore, the function $y = e^x+1$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y = -e^x,$ we get $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = -e^x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ -{e}^{x} & =-{e}^{x}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y = -e^x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y = 2e^x,$ we get $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2e^x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ 2{e}^{x} & =2{e}^{x}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y = 2e^x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to First-Order Differential Equations"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101795}}] :activity/title "Verifying Solutions of Differential Equations" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186101045}]}
